Latent Gaussian Models … with an example

Most commonly used models are actually LGMs (e.g., LM,LMM, GLM, GAM,etc)
A LGM consists of three elements:

The observation model \(\pi(\mathbf{y}|\eta,\theta) = \prod_{i=1}^n \pi(y_i|\eta_,\theta)\)
The latent Gaussian field \(\pi(\mathbf{u}|\theta)\)

The dependence structure

\[\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\beta_0 \\ \beta_1 \end{bmatrix}
&\sim \mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\mathrm{Q}^{-1}
\right)\\
\mathrm{Q} & =
\begin{bmatrix}
\tau_{\beta_0} & 0 \\ 0 & \tau_{\beta_1}
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}\]

Analysing de distribution of Ringlet butterfly in the UK
We analysed the distribution of the Ringlet butterfly (Aphantopus hyperantus) using \(&gt;\) 2 million records from 140,887 unique 1 km\(^2\) from the BNM programme between 1970 and 2014.

Occupancy model framework where

\[
\begin{aligned}
y_{ijt} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if Ringlet is on checklist } i \text{ on visit } j \text{ during year } t\\ 0 & \text{any species other than Ringlet is recorded}\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\]
